usage
=====

busybox provides the base userland and runit service manager. services
live in /etc/sv; enable one by linking it into /var/service, disable it
by removing the link:

  ln -s /etc/sv/<service> /var/service
  rm /var/service/<service>

available services:

  tty1..tty5    getty on virtual consoles
  ttyS0         getty on the first serial port
  mdev          hotplug daemon (instead of the kernel hotplug helper)
  udhcpc        dhcp client
  net-static    static ip configuration
  syslogd       system log, written to /var/log/messages
  klogd         kernel log to syslog
  crond         cron daemon (crontabs in /var/spool/cron/crontabs)
  ntpd          time sync with pool.ntp.org
  acpid         acpi event daemon
  backlight     restore/save screen brightness across reboots
  inetd         internet superserver (needs /etc/inetd.conf)
  httpd         small web server (serves the service dir by default,
                add '-h /path/to/www' in /etc/sv/httpd/run)

network services:

  udhcpc picks the first wireless interface unless IFACE is set in
  /etc/sv/udhcpc/conf. extra DNS servers can be set with DNS="...".

  net-static reads IFACE, IP, NETMASK and GATEWAY from
  /etc/sv/net-static/conf. it also overwrites /etc/resolv.conf using
  the gateway and 8.8.8.8 as nameservers.

  use only one of udhcpc, dhcpcd or net-static per interface.

suid commands:

  su, ping, ping6, traceroute, passwd, login, vlock, wall and crontab
  are routed through the setuid helper /bin/busybox-suidwrapper, so
  /bin/busybox itself stays non-setuid.
